WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.850
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:06.800 --> 00:00:09.380
AMRUTA JOSHI: My inspiration
has undoubtedly been my mother.

00:00:09.380 --> 00:00:14.000
She encouraged me to think
independently, to be curious,

00:00:14.000 --> 00:00:15.528
and to speak up for myself.

00:00:15.528 --> 00:00:17.570
ANU STRIVASTAVA: When I
started computer science,

00:00:17.570 --> 00:00:21.180
I was terrified of the
first classes I had to take.

00:00:21.180 --> 00:00:22.680
But I eventually
became really close

00:00:22.680 --> 00:00:24.560
with one of my
algorithms professors.

00:00:24.560 --> 00:00:28.550
And she inspired me to try
exploring out, doing projects

00:00:28.550 --> 00:00:30.980
on my own, and learning about
different fields in computer

00:00:30.980 --> 00:00:31.710
science.

00:00:31.710 --> 00:00:34.290
So when I go to speak at
conferences like the Google

00:00:34.290 --> 00:00:37.620
Cloud Summit, I really want to
make sure everyone there feels

00:00:37.620 --> 00:00:41.410
empowered and safe to ask
questions, take charge

00:00:41.410 --> 00:00:43.660
of their own careers, and
bring their new perspectives

00:00:43.660 --> 00:00:44.840
to the table.

00:00:44.840 --> 00:00:46.382
MAYA KACZOROWSKI:
I work in security.

00:00:46.382 --> 00:00:48.515
And so asking questions is
really, really critical.

00:00:48.515 --> 00:00:51.140
People who work in security come
from a variety of backgrounds,

00:00:51.140 --> 00:00:53.510
whether they come
from literature,

00:00:53.510 --> 00:00:56.890
from computer science, from a
marginalized group, whatever

00:00:56.890 --> 00:00:57.600
it happens to be.

00:00:57.600 --> 00:00:58.940
And they say, you know,
how do we actually make

00:00:58.940 --> 00:01:00.223
this work better for the user?

00:01:00.223 --> 00:01:02.390
If you're not doing that,
you're potentially missing

00:01:02.390 --> 00:01:03.770
out on a key part of your job.

00:01:03.770 --> 00:01:05.000
GRACE MOLLISON: One
manager in particular

00:01:05.000 --> 00:01:06.830
has always said to
me, look, I don't

00:01:06.830 --> 00:01:08.780
mind if you got a
problem, but I'd

00:01:08.780 --> 00:01:11.320
appreciate you actually having
thought that problem through

00:01:11.320 --> 00:01:12.530
and look and question it.

00:01:12.530 --> 00:01:14.738
Even if it's an area that's
not my area of expertise,

00:01:14.738 --> 00:01:17.300
I do try and actually look
around it at that problem,

00:01:17.300 --> 00:01:19.340
look around it to see
how I can deal with it.

00:01:19.340 --> 00:01:21.200
KATHY LEE: I became
a working mom.

00:01:21.200 --> 00:01:24.740
I work fearlessly day and night.

00:01:24.740 --> 00:01:26.960
When I worked for a
gentleman named Paul,

00:01:26.960 --> 00:01:30.770
there came an extraordinary
career opportunity.

00:01:30.770 --> 00:01:34.010
He encouraged me to step
out of my comfort zone

00:01:34.010 --> 00:01:36.680
and go for the bigger
career opportunities.

00:01:36.680 --> 00:01:38.720
ANAIS HAYES: When I
first joined Google,

00:01:38.720 --> 00:01:41.150
one of the very
first things I heard

00:01:41.150 --> 00:01:42.800
is a great quote from
Larry Page, which

00:01:42.800 --> 00:01:46.910
is about having a healthy
disregard for the impossible.

00:01:46.910 --> 00:01:49.340
And with a healthy disregard
for the impossible,

00:01:49.340 --> 00:01:51.215
people can do
anything, and I think

00:01:51.215 --> 00:01:52.340
that's incredibly powerful.

00:01:52.340 --> 00:01:55.390
[MUSIC PLAYING]

